   329	
   330	#### Known Divergences
   331	
   332	Both classes concern malformed input only. The vendored kernel bytes are pinned,
   333	so they are recorded, not repaired. Seven minimal repros are expected-failure
   334	fixtures with these reasons: one-step games (`episodeSteps` 2) whose first
   335	seat-0 action carries only the divergent field, so each diverges at line 1,
   336	step 0; the test fails if any stops diverging exactly there. Full-game policies
   337	exclude these inputs by default so a game can test the rest of the episode;
   338	`--include-known-divergences` restores them.
   339	
   340	The sweep classifies a divergence from the observed mismatch, not from the
   341	input alone. D2 requires a `rejected` record whose Python error is
   342	`TypeError: unhashable type`, an unhashable input on that line and a Rust
   343	`rust_accepted` result. D1 requires a Python-accepted transition with Unicode
   344	digit input on the divergent line, and a Rust recheck of the same trace with
   345	only that line's digits spelled in ASCII (what Python's `int()` read) must pass
   346	that line. Anything else is reported as a new divergence and the sweep exits 1;
   347	a trace corrupted at `public.day` on a D1 line stays unclassified.
   348	
   349	- **D1, Unicode decimal digits.** Python `int()` accepts non-ASCII decimal digit
   350	  strings such as `"\u0663"` (Arabic-Indic three) and `"\uff13"` (full-width
   351	  three). In PICKUP/PLACE counts Rust returns an error where Python acts; in
   352	  BUY_SEED, SELL, BUY_PRODUCT and BUY_ANIMAL quantities Rust drops the order
   353	  where Python executes three units. First found at step 13 of the free-hire
   354	  edge game (`BUY_PRODUCT FERTILIZER "\u0663"`, money 2,159 versus 2,460).
   355	- **D2, unhashable arrays/objects.** Python raises `TypeError`, so Kaggle's step
   356	  fails, when an array or object reaches a dict lookup: a unit verb
   357	  (`[["NORTH"]]`), a PLANT crop (also for a non-existent hand, via the atomic
   358	  seed check), a PICKUP or PLACE item, or a BUY_SEED or BUY_ANIMAL item. Rust
   359	  treats these as no-ops and accepts the step.
   360	
   361	All ASCII quantity spellings agree, including `" 4 "`, `"+2"`, `"0002"`,
   362	`"1_0"`, floats, booleans, `null`, arrays, 10^30 and uncaught-`int()` errors
   363	(`"abc"`, `null`, `[1]` counts), which both engines reject. Our policy's
   364	grammar emits only ASCII verbs, item names and integers, so neither class is
   365	reachable from model actions; an adapter accepting external actions would need
   366	explicit handling. The original failing full-game traces, their first
   367	divergences and the replay receipt are in
   368	`ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/1.1b/evidence/first-failing-games/`.
   369	
   370	Limits: generated games come from seeded random, edge and built-in policies,
   371	not strong play, and most random seats go bankrupt; the four official episodes
   372	remain the only recorded competitive worlds. Framework behavior outside the
   373	interpreter (timeouts, agent errors, `INVALID` statuses) is not modeled. The
   374	sweep is a bounded sample: 40 games and 303 probes, not exhaustive input
   375	coverage. A larger pod sweep remains open.
